✅ Common for: List, Set, Queue, Deque

Method	Description
boolean add(E e)	Adds element to collection
boolean addAll(Collection<? extends E> c)	Adds all elements from another collection
void clear()	Removes all elements
boolean contains(Object o)	Checks if element exists
boolean containsAll(Collection<?> c)	Checks if all elements exist
boolean isEmpty()	Checks if collection has 0 elements
Iterator<E> iterator()	Returns iterator
boolean remove(Object o)	Removes one element
boolean removeAll(Collection<?> c)	Removes all elements in given collection
boolean retainAll(Collection<?> c)	Keeps only given elements
int size()	Number of elements
Object[] toArray()	Converts to Object array
<T> T[] toArray(T[] a)	Converts to given array type
Stream<E> stream()	Returns sequential stream
Stream<E> parallelStream()	Returns parallel stream
Spliterator<E> spliterator()	For parallel traversal
📋 2️⃣ Interface — java.util.List<E>

(Extends Collection → ordered, allows duplicates)

➕ Additional methods:
Method	Description
void add(int index, E element)	Inserts at position
boolean addAll(int index, Collection<? extends E> c)	Inserts a collection at index
E get(int index)	Get element by position
E set(int index, E element)	Replace element at index
E remove(int index)	Remove by index
int indexOf(Object o)	First occurrence index
int lastIndexOf(Object o)	Last occurrence index
ListIterator<E> listIterator()	Iterator that can move both directions
ListIterator<E> listIterator(int index)	Starts at given index
List<E> subList(int from, int to)	Sub-view of list
🪣 Common Implementations:
🔹 ArrayList

Dynamic array internally.

Fast random access.

Slower insert/remove in middle.

Extra methods:
None beyond List.

🔹 LinkedList

Doubly linked list.

Also implements Deque.

Extra methods (from Deque):

Method	Description
void addFirst(E e)	Add at head
void addLast(E e)	Add at tail
E getFirst()	Get first element
E getLast()	Get last element
E removeFirst()	Remove first
E removeLast()	Remove last
E peekFirst()	Peek first
E peekLast()	Peek last
🧱 3️⃣ Interface — java.util.Set<E>

(Extends Collection → unique elements, unordered)

Methods same as Collection, but no positional ones.
🔹 HashSet

Backed by HashMap.

Uses hashCode() & equals() to ensure uniqueness.

No extra methods beyond Set.

🔹 LinkedHashSet

Maintains insertion order.

Also backed by LinkedHashMap.

🔹 TreeSet

Sorted set (Red-Black tree internally).

Uses compareTo() or Comparator.

Extra methods (from NavigableSet):

Method	Description
E first()	First element
E last()	Last element
E lower(E e)	Greatest element < e
E floor(E e)	≤ e
E ceiling(E e)	≥ e
E higher(E e)	> e
SortedSet<E> subSet(E from, E to)	Range view
NavigableSet<E> descendingSet()	Reverse view
Iterator<E> descendingIterator()	Reverse iterator
